#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116144} :activity/id #uuid "d1104d6d-49bb-56ce-9458-47abd56e4785" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050900 :topic/id #uuid "6f8cc60e-3ff3-5a90-b875-9b86295e501d" :topic/math-academy-id 1734} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116144 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116502 :tutorial/content "We can find the inverse of a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $A$ using elementary row operations on the augmented matrix \n\n$$\n[A\\, |\\, I],\n$$\n\n \nwhere $I$ is the $3 \\times 3$ identity matrix.\nWe do this by transforming the left-hand side of the augmented matrix into the identity matrix, which gives an augmented matrix of the form \n\n$$\n[ I\\, |\\, A^{-1}].\n$$\n\nAs it turns out, this method works for any invertible matrix!\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find the inverse of the $4\\times 4$ matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/15/159ac4bad9c080651266ed6cc2369149c38c38dfb276b27e6c6dccea6d7601c9.svg)\n\nWe write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$, as shown below. Then, we apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n![](images/24/2470c145390237d588db20950bdf573f79961929bbf2051e8f4a067b977ddc68.svg)\n\nTherefore, the inverse matrix is\n![](images/64/6441521c48a9d1ba632eda037531749ee41454b7c616d5d88bf522a4b2dcd458.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "155c4e79-e15d-5e2b-88eb-14556fd8e7f2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3172 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "418c7a1f-9223-51fd-97f4-1803f5d7ca05" :step/math-academy-id 657175 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116145}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116145 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115256 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115085 :question/id #uuid "0d75d1f6-d739-5548-b2a4-66b34c59fead" :question/math-academy-id "e-4735"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b82b13ea-c703-5c17-b20d-9e24c061e57a" :knowledge-point/key-pr